<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Zande Kingdoms</h1><p class="st-meta">1750 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A cluster of kingdoms ruled by the Avongara aristocracy across the Nile–Congo watershed, made famous by Evans-Pritchard's study of Zande witchcraft and oracles.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From the 18th century the Ambomu people, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avongara">Avongara</a> ruling clan, expanded east and south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbomou-river">Mbomu</a> river, conquering and assimilating many neighbouring peoples. As each Avongara ruler's sons founded their own domains, a patchwork of related but independent kingdoms emerged across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uele-river">Uele</a> and Mbomu basins.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each king ruled a central court and appointed sons and loyal commoners as provincial governors, keeping organised companies of young warriors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Zande</a> society relied on farming and hunting and was noted for its use of poison oracles to settle disputes and identify witches. In the 19th century Zande kings fought Arab-Sudanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slave traders and, later, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdist-state">Mahdist state</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdoms were partitioned between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, French <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ubangi-shari">Ubangi-Shari</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/anglo-egyptian-sudan">Anglo-Egyptian Sudan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gbudwe">Gbudwe</a>, the most powerful king, was killed resisting British forces in 1905. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-e-evans-pritchard">E. E. Evans-Pritchard's</a> 1937 study of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Azande</a> became a classic of anthropology.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1750 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:94.53%;width:3.08%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1750 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gbudwe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Zande religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Zande</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, <a href="/countries/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>, <a href="/countries/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Azande, Gbudwe's kingdom, Avongara kingdoms</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1828&amp;focus=zande-kingdoms" aria-label="Zande Kingdoms on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5342 2241 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zande Kingdoms lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5362 2652h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5662 2497h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5662 2497h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Democratic Republic of the Congo. <a href="/atlas?year=1828&amp;focus=zande-kingdoms">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

